Instead of our classic view of a single point of execution within a program
(a single program counter or instruction pointer where instructions are
being fetched from and executed), a "multi-threaded" program has more than
one point of execution (multiple program counter or instruction pointer
that each of which is being fetched from and executed).

Another way to think of multi-thread concept is that each thread is like
a _separate process_, except that these threads _share_ the same address
space and thus can access the _same_ data.

The state of a single thread is very similar to that of a process. The
thread has a program counter (PC) or instruction pointer (IP) that
tracks where the program is fetching instructions from. Each thread has
its _own private_ set of registers it uses for computation. Thus, if there
are two threads that are running on a single processor, when switching from
the running thread (T1) to run another thread (T2), a "context switch" must
take place.

The "context switch" between threads is quite similar to the context switch
between processes, as the register state of the running thread (T1) must be
saved and the register state of the soon-to-be-running thread (T2) restored
before running the soon-to-be-running thread (T2). The major difference
of "context switch" between threads and processes is that the context switch
between threads use the _same_ address space, while the context switch
between process use _different_ address space. So there's no need to switch
page table when we do context switch between threads.

With processes, we saved the process' state (such as registers value,
program counter, etc) to a process control block (PCB). With threads, we saved
the thread's state (such as registers value, program counter, etc) to a thread
control block (TCB).

In a classic process (or single-threaded process), there is a single stack.
However, in a multi-threaded process, each thread runs independently and call
into various functions to do whatever work it needs to do. So a multi-threaded
process has multiple stacks, one stack per thread (a.k.a thread-local storage).

Why use threads?
  - Parallelism, speeding up the process by using multiple processors to each
    perform a portion of the work.
  - To avoid blocking program's progress due to slow I/O. While one thread in
    our program waits, the CPU scheduler can switch to another thread which is
    ready to run and do some work.
(In above cases, we could also use multiple processes instead of threads.
However, threads share an address space which make sharing data easier.
Processes are a more sound choice for logically separate tasks where little
_sharing_ of data structures in memory is needed.)

2 possibilities of running threads:
  - Thread 1 running first, and then Thread 2 running after Thread 1:
    - Thread 2 running immediately.
    - Thread 2 put on hold until we trigger it (?).
  - Thread 1 and Thread 2 running at the same time.

A "critical section" is a piece of code that accesses a shared resource (like
a variable or data structure) and must not be concurrently executed by more than
one thread.

A "mutual exclusion" property guarantees that if one thread is executing within
the critical section, other threads will be prevented to execute those critical
section too.

The idea behind making a series of instructions "atomic" is simply expressed
with the phrase "all or nothing", either the instruction has run to completion
or not run at all, there is not in-between state.

Sometimes the grouping of many instructions into a single atomic instruction is
called "transaction", an idea developed in detail in the world of databases and
transaction processing.

A "race condition" arise if multiple threads of execution enter the "critical
section" at roughly the _same time_, both attempt to update the shared data
structure which usually lead to unexpected result.

Subtle difference between "concurrency" and "parallelism":
  - Concurrency means that multiple processes are active concurrently (at the
    same time). We can do concurrency with one processor (with time-sharing
    concept, switching between active processes) or multiple processors. Just
    because it is active, does not mean it is currently running (?).
  - Parallelism refers to the _actual_ execution of instructions from multiple
    processes or threads at the same time on _different_ processors. In order
    to achieve parallelism, we need multiple processors that are active at the
    same time.
